.TH CAT 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
cat \- concatenate files to standard output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cat
.RB [ \-nbvteAu ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cat
writes the contents of each
.I file
to standard output, in order.
With no
.I file
operands, or for a
.I file
named
.BR \- ,
it reads standard input.
A
.I file
that cannot be opened is reported on standard error and skipped, and the
remaining files are still written.
A read error ends that file.
It is reported only without options, and with
.B \-n
or
.B \-b
alone it does not affect the exit status.
.PP
Without options, the bytes are copied unchanged.
The options number the lines, show nonprinting characters, or both.
.SS Line numbers
With
.B \-n
or
.BR \-b ,
a numbered line starts with its number, right-aligned in 6 columns,
and a tab.
Numbering starts at 1 and continues across files.
.PP
Without
.BR \-v ,
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-e ,
or
.BR \-A ,
the input is read as lines of text, which changes it: a CR before an LF
is removed, a NUL byte ends a line and is removed, and every line is
written with a final LF, including the last line of a file that has
none.
.PP
With any of them, the number is followed by two spaces instead of a
tab, and lines end only at LF: a CR or NUL is shown like any other byte.
A file that does not end with an LF then runs into the first line of the
next file, which gets no number.
.SS Nonprinting characters
With
.BR \-v ,
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-e ,
or
.BR \-A ,
each byte is written as follows, so the output is plain ASCII:
.IP \(bu 2
LF and the printable ASCII characters 0x20 to 0x7E are written as
themselves, and so is tab, unless
.B \-t
is given.
.IP \(bu 2
Any other byte below 0x20 is
.B \(ha
followed by the character 0x40 above it, from
.B \(ha@
to
.BR \(ha_ ,
so CR is
.B \(haM
and ESC is
.BR \(ha[ .
DEL, 0x7F, is
.BR \(ha? .
.IP \(bu 2
A byte from 0x80 to 0xFF is
.B M\-
followed by the byte 0x80 below it, written as itself if printable and
in
.B \(ha
form otherwise, so 0x8A is
.B M\-\(haJ
and 0xFF is
.BR M\-\(ha? .
Text in UTF-8 appears byte by byte: the encoding of
.B e
with an acute accent, 0xC3 0xA9, is
.BR M\-CM\-) .
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-vn ,
and may follow
.I file
operands.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.TP
.B \-n
Number every output line, as described under
.BR "Line numbers" .
.TP
.B \-b
Number only nonempty lines.
Empty lines are written without a number and are not counted.
Without
.BR \-v ,
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-e ,
or
.BR \-A ,
a line holding only a CR is empty.
Overrides
.BR \-n .
.TP
.B \-v
Show nonprinting characters, as described under
.BR "Nonprinting characters" .
.TP
.B \-t
Like
.BR \-v ,
and also show each tab as
.BR \(haI .
.TP
.B \-e
Like
.BR \-v ,
and also write
.B $
before each LF.
A last line without an LF gets no
.BR $ .
.TP
.B \-A
Same as
.BR \-vte .
.TP
.B \-u
Accepted for compatibility and ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, the first
.I file
operand ends the options, so that later arguments such as
.B \-n
are file names.
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, as described in
.BR NOTES ,
and the console interprets the bytes in its own code page.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option, a
.I file
could not be opened or read, or standard output could not be written.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe that was closed before all output was written.
.SH EXAMPLES
Reassemble a file cut into pieces by
.BR split :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cat backup.zip.?? >backup.zip
.EE
.RE
.PP
Number the lines of a source file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cat \-n main.c
.EE
.RE
.PP
Reveal CR line endings, tabs, and trailing spaces:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aqone\er\entwo\etthree \en\(aq | cat \-A
one\(haM$
two\(haIthree $
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write a file from a here-document:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cat >config.h <<EOF
> #define VERSION "1.0"
> EOF
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU cat:
.IP \(bu 2
There are no long options, and no
.BR \-E ,
.BR \-T ,
or
.BR \-s .
.IP \(bu 2
With
.B \-n
or
.B \-b
alone, line endings change, as described under
.BR "Line numbers" .
.IP \(bu 2
With
.BR \-v ,
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-e ,
or
.BR \-A ,
line numbers are followed by two spaces, not a tab.
.PP
Standard input and output are binary, so without options CR LF line
endings and Ctrl-Z characters are copied unchanged.
.PP
File names may use drive letters and backslashes.
They may contain any Unicode characters on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, and are limited to the system code page on earlier versions.
The device names
.IR /dev/null ,
.IR /dev/zero ,
.IR /dev/urandom ,
.IR /dev/stdin ,
.I /dev/tty
(the console), and
.I /dev/fd/N
work as on Unix.
A directory operand fails with
.BR "Permission denied" ,
or with
.B \-n
or
.B \-b
alone,
.BR "Is a directory" .
.PP
Lines typed at a console arrive with CR LF line endings.
To end the input, type Ctrl-Z at the start of a line and press Enter.
Ctrl-D is an ordinary character.
.PP
When standard output is a console, text is converted for display from
the ANSI code page, which is UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 and later.
Escape sequences, such as those for color, take effect, and busybox
emulates them on consoles without virtual terminal support.
Without options, output is written in blocks as it is read, and a block
that contains a NUL byte is written as is, with no conversion or
emulation.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR dos2unix (1),
.BR head (1),
.BR less (1),
.BR nl (1),
.BR split (1),
.BR tac (1),
.BR tee (1),
.BR xxd (1)
